WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:05.110 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  my days as an assistant principal began  by the flagpole welcoming students to  school like Roberto with his rat tail  and ninja turtle backpack he was just  one of those kiddos who made me smile  I'd greet students with high fives fist  bumps and as Roberto taught me these  super cool sliding handshake fist bump  then I would usher them through the  doors thinking that once we were all  inside we could get down to the business  of learning and growing up unaffected by  the outside world I became a teacher  because of students like Roberto I knew  that education could open doors  I learned however that a community can  change a school today  I want to introduce you to Mitchell  middle school I want to introduce you to  Mitchell because it introduced me to  challenges I still grapple with it  transformed my understanding of  community and education to get to  Mitchell you go to the trendiest  neighborhood in town then head just four  blocks to the east the spin studios and  breweries quickly shift to small homes  vacant lots and the Dollar Tree inside  the building our students like most  teenagers grappled with their first  essay saying Selena Gomez at break  outside the school they helped care for  little siblings worried about the  deportation of a family member  drug  sometimes to find food in an area with  no grocery store my kids carried trauma  and uncertainties with them that I the  adult had never known  Mitchell taught me that the brick walls  of a school building  do not make a fortress schools and their  surrounding communities are constantly  shaping and being shaped by one another  with Mitchell as our model let's zoom  out where is the opportunity gap in  American schools we know that if high  school graduates who grew up in poverty  14% only will graduate college within  eight years we know that third grade  reading scores are used to predict  future needed prison capacity now let's  zoom in to our current home Palo Alto  the green represents wealth a to Vinick  Elementary School just six percent of  students qualify for free and reduced  lunch a measure of financial need  students here are three point six years  above the national average in their  learning at Los Robles Ronald McNair  Academy just one mile away 93% of  students qualify for free reduced lunch  students here students here are nearly  three grade levels below the national  average how is this possible that there  is a sixth grade level gap in our one  zip code  let's talk about funding when I was a  teacher a rainy day could mean that I  taught verbs while maneuvering around a  bucket to catch water dripping through  the roof recess was on a cracked parking  lot these were the physical  representations of a system that provide  the fortunate with boundless opportunity  while leaving behind far too many  children across the country school  districts that served mostly minority  children receive 23 billion dollars less  than majority white districts in wealthy  areas like Palo Alto property taxes  provide an infusion of money  parent-teacher associations are powerful  and can bring in further funding we must  acknowledge these facts and their  implications at the same time they're  more than just numbers we the people  must get to know one another ed Mitchell  our students brought the staff homemade  tamales chaperoned the eighth grade  formal arrived at 6:30 in the morning  before work to conference with teachers  they cared deeply but they did not  always have the pocketbook to meet every  need our time for school is ending soon  but our connection to schools is not  today's students are your future  colleagues employees and fellow voters  the issues in publication in public  education are yes messy and bureaucratic  I cannot say they are easy to fix I can  say they are not permanent too often  glitzy innovations like a new education  technology tool dominate our attention  these ideas are great and they can help  but the first step is far simpler and  rooted in our daily lives figure out how  you connect to  the community around you and respond  Mitchell taught me three ways that we  can begin this process by thinking about  our place our resources and our  influence let's start with where you are  your place I challenge you to expand the  definition of your community look beyond  you're familiar blocks learn about the  area get to know schools like Mitchell  if you have children send them to public  school don't opt out if you want your  kid to be successful in the real world  send them to school there they will  benefit from getting to learn from their  classmates and being part of the  community their community secondly the  most tangible resources since the  majority of public school funding comes  from property taxes schools and property  poor neighborhoods need further access  to resources find a school near you that  would benefit from whatever you can  contribute that might mean corporate  donations or spare office chairs lastly  how can we use our influence the good  news is that education is a unicorn one  of those rare areas where members of  both political parties can find  agreement across the country nearly  every school board is publicly elected  yet not even one in ten people vote in  local school board elections participate  to support candidates who extend  equitable funding to schools and  programs I would love if every single  one of you left here today and decided  to become a middle school teacher  I get it this is business school no  matter where you go after you leave  Stanford remember that the brick walls  of a school are not a fortress our  communities will shape our schools and  our schools will shape our communities  how will you contribute Roberto is now  in eighth grade I do not know how his  story will unfold I do know the  statistics tell me he has a 60% chance  of graduating high school he is more  likely to become incarcerated than to  graduate from college but I have hope  for Roberto I have hope because of  students like Michael Michael was in my  first year teaching he was one of those  kids who carried a second-hand briefcase  to school in sixth grade  fortunately Michael is accepted to a  local scholarship and mentorship program  that supported him through high school  Michael is now a freshman at Brown nape  majoring in neuroscience and education  because as Michael told me he wants to  return and give back to his community  Michael is the young man he is today  because of his hard work personal gifts  and the support of a community that  carried him forward with our collective  efforts  let's make students like Michael no  longer the exception thank you  you  [Music]

